The laser book (route 'laser-study', layout sections/laser-study.html). 'chapters' is a list; each has an 'id', a 'title', an optional 'intro' and 'sections'. Each section has an 'id', a 'title', a 'text' (the easy version), an optional 'picture' and an optional 'deeper' with its own 'title' and 'text' (the full version, shown by the 'Dig deeper' button; leave 'deeper' out and the button goes too). The page writes the numbers (1, 1.1, 1.2 ...): never put numbers in a title, and the contents renumber themselves when chapters or sections move. PICTURES: each 'picture' has 'image' (a file name, e.g. 'images/study/ruby.png'), 'alt' (what the picture shows, for screen readers), 'caption' (the line under it) and 'todo' (what the picture should show). While 'image' is empty the page shows the 'todo' in a dashed box; put the file in 'image' and the box goes, the picture and its caption appear. TEXT: an empty line (

) starts a new paragraph, **two stars** make words bold, and every formula, symbol or number with a unit is LaTeX between dollar signs: $E = h
u$ inside a sentence, or $$ ... $$ as a paragraph of its own. In JSON every backslash of LaTeX is written twice ( rac). Keep the formulas of 'hy' and 'en' the same and in the same order; inside a formula the decimal point is a point in both languages. A formula must stay on one line and hold no ** or [..](..). Titles, pictures' words and the sources hold no formulas. A link to a chapter is [words](#chapter-pumping) and to a section [words](#section-ruby): # then chapter- or section- then its id; the link's words hold no numbers. A person, place, material or device a reader may not know is linked, at its first mention in a chapter, to Wikipedia: [Theodore Maiman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Maiman) in 'en'; in 'hy' to the Armenian article (https://hy.wikipedia.org/wiki/...) when there is one, else to the same English one. Write a space in the address as _ and brackets as %28 and %29 (a ')' would end the link); only en.wikipedia.org, hy.wikipedia.org and, where there is no article, a photo on commons.wikimedia.org are allowed. Such a link opens in a new tab. The Armenian is a first translation, to be read by a fluent speaker.
